# 19. Evaluation Metrics

The paper considers multiple evaluation metrics.

## Accuracy

```text
Accuracy =
Correct Predictions / Total Predictions
```

Accuracy tells us how many predictions are correct overall.

However, because the dataset is highly imbalanced, accuracy alone can be misleading.

---

## Recall

For a particular class:

```text
Recall = TP / (TP + FN)
```

It measures how many actual instances of that class were correctly identified.

---

## Balanced Accuracy

The paper defines balanced accuracy as the macro-average of recall across the three classes:

```text
Balanced Accuracy =
(Recall_Normal + Recall_Suspect + Recall_Pathological) / 3
```

This gives equal importance to all three classes.

That is why it is particularly appropriate for this problem.

---

## Balanced Error Rate

The paper defines:

```text
Balanced Error Rate =
1 − Balanced Accuracy
```

Therefore, if balanced accuracy increases, balanced error rate decreases.

---

# 20. Why Balanced Accuracy Is Important for Our Project

This is one of the most important ideas we take from the base paper.

Our dataset contains:

```text
Normal       → 77.8%
Suspect      → 13.9%
Pathological → 8.3%
```

Therefore, a model could perform well on the majority Normal class while performing poorly on the minority classes.

Balanced accuracy forces us to consider the recall of all classes equally.

For our project, this is particularly relevant because the goal is not simply:

> "How many predictions are correct?"

It is also:

> "How well can the system recognize each fetal health category?"

