# 56. Important Viva Questions From the Base Paper

## Q1. Why did you select this paper as your base paper?

**Answer:**

We selected it because it directly addresses fetal health classification using CTG data and provides a complete machine-learning pipeline involving preprocessing, class-imbalance handling using SMOTE, multiple classification algorithms, cross-validation and balanced-accuracy-based evaluation. These components provide a strong methodological foundation for our proposed project.

---

## Q2. What is the major problem addressed by the paper?

**Answer:**

The paper addresses automatic classification of fetal health conditions from CTG data, with particular emphasis on the class imbalance present in the dataset.

---

## Q3. Why is the dataset imbalanced?

**Answer:**

The three fetal health conditions do not occur equally in the dataset. Normal cases form the majority, while Suspect and Pathological cases are minority classes.

---

## Q4. Why is accuracy alone not enough?

**Answer:**

Because the dataset is imbalanced. A model can obtain high overall accuracy by performing well on the majority Normal class while performing poorly on minority classes. Balanced accuracy gives equal importance to the recall of all classes.

---

## Q5. What is SMOTE?

**Answer:**

SMOTE stands for Synthetic Minority Oversampling Technique. It generates synthetic minority-class samples by interpolating between existing minority observations and their nearest minority-class neighbors.

---

## Q6. Why did the paper use SMOTE?

**Answer:**

The paper selected SMOTE because it generates synthetic samples instead of simply duplicating minority samples, preserves majority-class information unlike undersampling, and showed more consistent performance improvements than class weighting in the preliminary experiments described by the authors.

---

## Q7. Why should SMOTE not be applied to the test set?

**Answer:**

The test set should represent unseen data. Applying SMOTE to the test set can contaminate the evaluation process and produce an unrealistic estimate of model performance.

---

## Q8. What is 5-fold cross-validation?

**Answer:**

The dataset is divided into five folds. During each iteration, one fold is used for testing and the remaining four folds are used for training. This is repeated five times so that every fold serves as the test fold once.

---

## Q9. What is the difference between SMOTE and cross-validation?

**Answer:**

SMOTE addresses class imbalance, while cross-validation provides a more robust method of evaluating model performance. They solve different problems.

---

## Q10. Which model performed best in the base paper?

**Answer:**

LightGBM achieved the highest reported balanced accuracy among the traditional ML models, with **91.34% balanced accuracy under 5-fold cross-validation**.

---

## Q11. What was the highest standard accuracy reported?

**Answer:**

LightGBM achieved **95.67% accuracy** under the 80/20 train-test evaluation.

---

## Q12. Why did the paper investigate DNN?

**Answer:**

The authors wanted to determine whether a deep learning approach could capture complex patterns in CTG data and provide better performance than traditional machine-learning models.

---

## Q13. Did DNN outperform the traditional ML models?

**Answer:**

No. The paper reports that, given the limited size of the medical dataset, the investigated DNN did not outperform the traditional machine-learning approaches. The authors suggest that larger and more diverse datasets may make deep learning more advantageous.

---

## Q14. What is the main gap that your project addresses?

**Answer:**

Our project extends the base-paper methodology by bringing together optimization, ensemble learning and explainability into a single framework for AI-assisted fetal health risk assessment.

---

## Q15. Are you claiming that your project is already better than the base paper?

**Answer:**

No. The base paper provides our baseline. We can claim improvement only after conducting controlled experiments and demonstrating improvement using appropriate evaluation metrics.

